DomainRecon Source-Available License, version 1.0

Copyright (c) 2025-2026 W4ll-I. All rights reserved.

The English text below is the binding version. A French translation, for
convenience only, follows it.

1. What you may do, without asking

   a) Read, study and audit the source code.
   b) Download the official releases of DomainRecon and run them, for your own
      use, to audit domains and systems that you own or are explicitly
      authorized to test.
   c) Report problems and propose changes (issues, pull requests, patches)
      to the copyright holder, under section 4.

2. What you may NOT do without prior written permission

   a) Use, copy or reuse the source code, in whole or in part, in another
      project, product, script or service.
   b) Sell, rent, sublicense, host as a service, or otherwise make money
      from DomainRecon, its code, or anything derived from it.
   c) Modify the code for any other purpose than preparing a contribution
      under section 4, or distribute a modified version (including a public
      fork that is distributed, published or deployed).
   d) Duplicate or redistribute the source code or the releases, in any form
      and even for free: no public fork, mirror, re-upload, repackaging or
      sharing with third parties. Only the copies needed for your own use
      (your devices, your backups) are allowed.
   e) Remove or alter copyright notices, this license, or the origin of the
      software.
   f) File, or help anyone file, a patent, utility model, design right or
      other registration of an industrial property right that covers
      DomainRecon, its code, its design or its logo, or anything derived from
      them. Any right obtained in breach of this clause is, to the extent
      permitted by law, held for the copyright holder and must be transferred
      to them on request.

   Permission may be requested from the copyright holder. A permission is
   valid only if it is written, names what it allows, and comes from the
   copyright holder.

3. Responsible use

   DomainRecon contains active modules (port scan, subdomain takeover checks
   and others). You are solely responsible for having the right to test every
   target. Scanning systems without authorization may be illegal. Nothing in
   this license authorizes any scan.

4. Contributions

   If you send a contribution (code, text, translation, data), you confirm
   that you have the right to do so, and you grant the copyright holder a
   perpetual, worldwide, free, irrevocable and sublicensable license to use,
   modify and distribute it, in DomainRecon and in any other work. The copyright
   holder is free to accept, change or refuse any contribution. Sending a
   contribution gives you no right on DomainRecon.

5. Third-party components

   DomainRecon includes components written by others, each under its own
   license (see THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md). This license does not apply to them
   and does not reduce the rights you have on them.

6. No warranty, no liability

   DomainRecon is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. The
   copyright holder is not liable for any damage, loss of data, or legal
   consequence resulting from its use, to the extent permitted by law.

7. Termination

   Any breach of section 2 ends your rights under this license
   automatically. You must then stop using and delete your copies of the code
   and of anything derived from it.

8. Nature of this license

   This is a "source-available" license: the code is visible, but it is not
   free software nor open source in the sense of the Open Source Definition.
   All rights not expressly granted are reserved. If you publish this project
   on a platform whose terms give users some rights over public repositories
   (for example the right to view and to fork inside that platform), those
   rights come from the platform's terms and stop there.

